< November an addition in square brackets was made to the first of the paragraphs added late in the evening of 27 November, on what Lean has since accepted about shares; a fresh instance read it 
< against the Lean statements. A second visitor read a first version (`check-visit/visit-2.md`), and a fresh instance read that version against the page it replaces and against the records 
< (`check-visit/report-visit.md`): it required seven changes, for my account again claimed more than the records show, as "What the reviews caught" tells of earlier readings. The sections were then 
< rewritten, read by a third visitor (`check-visit/visit-3.md`) and by the same instance (`check-visit/report-visit-2.md`), which found the seven changes made and required three more, to the paragraph 
< on checking and to one link; and rewritten again. A fourth visitor read that version (`check-visit/visit-4.md`), and the same instance read every sentence that was new or changed in it 
< (`check-visit/report-visit-3.md`): it found the three changes made and every link right, and required four changes more: to a sentence after the table that my own search did not bear out (one pattern 
< on the clock of 12 hours takes 18 marks, not 12 at most), to a sentence of "In short" that could be read as false, to one word of "How it was found", and to this section's account of what is kept 
< from the old page. The sections were rewritten once more, and the same instance read what was new or changed (`check-visit/report-visit-4.md`): it found the four changes made, the sentence after the 
< table borne out by its own search and by mine run again to 18 marks, and every link right; it required one change more, to a sentence of "How it was found" that said two programs had settled all 637 
< patterns when one alone had settled 44 of them (the wording had been its own suggestion), and it corrected the first sentence of this account. Both are made in its words, with a wording that it 
< suggested for the count of patterns after the table, and have not been read again. No visitor has read the page since the fourth. The sections from "The rule, in formulas" to "For those who want to 
< check" are cut from the page as it was, word for word, the first of them under a new heading and with the old page's paragraph "For mathematicians" put at its end, and with twenty-six paragraphs 
< added on 27 and 28 November to "What came after, in full", each group marked there with the word "Added" (three on markings of at most 36 marks, three on every marking, two on the Lean proof, five on 
< clocks with an even number of hours, four late in the evening, five on 28 November and four later that day, which say themselves who read them; of the first three: the instance that had refereed the 
< note behind them twice read a first draft, one paragraph, against that note and its own reports and required four changes; a stranger, a fresh instance that read only that section, could not follow 
< the draft, and it was rewritten as three paragraphs with an example; the referee read the three against the note and its reports and required three changes more, which are made in its words; the 
< stranger read them too, could follow the example but not yet the step from it to the general rule, and they were changed once more: the example now goes one digit further and the rule is told more 
< shortly; the referee read the text as changed, found the example right in both its steps by a check of its own, and required two changes more, to the sentence that carries the example over to other 
< clocks and to the reason why few places stay; they are made in its words, and nobody has read the text since; the stranger's two reports are in `check-small/`, the referee's with the note); they were 
< read against the records as they themselves tell, at the end of "What came after, in full" and in "For those who want to check". Where they speak of the two sections "What came after" and of readings 
< of both, the first of the two is a paragraph of the old page that the three items above have replaced. "What stands behind it" begins with "Then" because it used to follow "How it was found". No 
< longer on the page: the old title; the last three sentences of the old byline; the old opening of "The question", with its caption; the second half of the old heading of "The answer"; some clauses of 
< "How it was found" (among them the letters p and q, the count "of the 554 patterns", and the claim that I guessed before my search programs had run on any clock but the one of 6 hours, with its 
< caution in parentheses about an earlier table; the claim is the one "What the reviews caught" tells of); and that paragraph. The page as it was is kept as `README.before-visit.md`. The picture was 
< drawn again for a narrow screen by `clock_fig2.py`, which computes the centre of gravity; `clock_fig.py` drew the earlier, wider one, `clock-five-marks.wide-before-27nov.png`. The table for the clock 
< of 12 hours is from `table12/table12.py`, a plain search. My first run of it stopped at 12 marks (`table12/table12.to-12-marks.out`), and from it I wrote that no pattern needs more; the reader's own 
